    "text": "Twisted pair cable is made of pairs of insulated copper wires twisted around each other; the twisting reduces crosstalk (interference where a signal in one wire leaks into a neighbouring wire). Unshielded twisted pair (UTP) has no extra shielding and is the cheap, common LAN (Local Area Network) cable graded in categories such as Cat 5 (and Cat 5e) and Cat 6, the higher categories supporting faster speeds. A twisted pair network cable ends in an RJ-45 (Registered Jack 45) connector. Shielded twisted pair (STP) adds a foil or braided shield to protect against electromagnetic interference, but costs more. Twisted pair is cheap and easy to install, but suffers attenuation over long distances and has limited bandwidth. Coaxial cable has a central copper conductor, surrounded by an insulator (dielectric), a braided metal shield, and an outer plastic jacket. On networks it is fitted with a BNC (Bayonet Neill-Concelman) connector; on TV and satellite decoders it uses a screw-on F connector. Thin coaxial (thinnet) is flexible and used in LANs; thick coaxial (thicknet) is stiffer and carries signals further. Coaxial is more resistant to interference and has higher bandwidth than twisted pair, but is bulkier and more expensive; it is still used for TV aerials and satellite dishes."
